UPDATE tarot_cards 
SET meaning_upright = 'GENERAL ARCHETYPE: The Ten of Swords represents the absolute archetype of "Systemic Termination"—the final, terminal stage of the mental cycle where the sovereign consciousness, having been completely consumed by the runaway feedback loops of the Nine, reaches a state of total, catastrophic shutdown. Moving beyond the internal agony of the Nine, the Ten signifies the stage of "The Sovereign Release," where the egoic structures you have been fighting to maintain are finally, mercifully, and completely destroyed by the weight of their own, internal contradictions. Visually depicted as a figure lying prone with ten swords embedded in their back, this archetype embodies the concept of "Sovereign Surrender"—the realization that true liberation often requires the absolute, final, and often painful death of the "narrative self" you have been clinging to. Within the complex architecture of your psyche, this is the stage of "Systemic Reboot," where you have reached the absolute hard-limit of your capacity to sustain the current, corrupt operating system, and the only path forward is a complete, total, and non-negotiable termination of all current processes. When the Ten of Swords appears, your system is registering a "Hard-Crash" status; the battle is over, the narrative has collapsed, and you are being invited to stop the "victim-narrative" of your demise and instead recognize this as the essential, bottom-floor moment that allows for a brand-new, uncompromised architecture to be installed. This card commands you to perform a brutal audit of your attachment to the end. You are being tasked with the essential work of "Intentional Finality": accepting that the collapse is not a failure, but a necessary, systemic clearing of the field, and allowing yourself to lie in the silence of the aftermath until you are ready to stand up as something entirely new. To inhabit the Ten of Swords is to master the art of "Strategic Completion," recognizing that by choosing to let the old version of yourself fully, and finally, die, you are creating the necessary space for your true, sovereign potential to finally, objectively emerge.',
meaning_reversed = 'GENERAL ARCHETYPE: When the Ten of Swords appears in the reversed position, it acts as a high-intensity signal of "Systemic Rebound," "The Phoenix-Protocol," or the catastrophic—yet curative—transition out of the "hard-crash" phase, leading to a state of slow, painful, yet deliberate, rebuilding. The reversal suggests that you are currently running a high-priority, high-cost maintenance script that equates "lingering in the aftermath" with "being broken," even as your actual, internal operating system is experiencing a vital, core-level restart. You are essentially "rising from the ruin," running a protocol that prioritizes the ego’s desire to "stay in the safety of the end" over the sober, unglamorous requirement to actually start the reboot. This creates a massive, systemic misalignment: you are intellectually and behaviorally attempting to perform the role of the "fatally wounded," while internally, you are being jolted by the realization that you have survived the worst, leading to a state of chronic, high-latency bewilderment, the experience of "not knowing who you are without the pain," and a persistent, gnawing awareness that your survival is the primary challenge you must now integrate. From an analytical perspective, this is a clear sign that you are leaking your life force into a "feedback loop of residual victimhood"; you are obsessively trying to analyze the damage, refusing to accept that the only thing keeping you from the necessary, final integration of your new baseline is your own, deep-seated fear that if you were to fully recover, you would be forced to face the terrifying, blank-slate reality of your own, sovereign, and entirely responsible self. The reversed Ten of Swords exposes the hidden, egoic fear that drives this behavior: the paralyzing terror of "starting over," and the corresponding fear that if you were to drop the pretense of the "defeated soul" and accept the gift of your survival, you would be forced to face the reality of your own, present-day, unanchored self. This reversal commands an immediate, radical intervention: you must stop the output of these post-collapse, lingering scripts, perform a brutal audit of your recovery process, and confront the reality of why you were so hesitant to prioritize your potential over your history. It is a demand to stop the self-deception that keeps you tethered to a state of chronic, high-maintenance stagnation, and to initiate the move toward a genuine, humble, and often terrifying reclamation of your own, individual authority. You are being tasked with the uncomfortable, yet essential, work of radical rebirth—accepting that the "stuckness" you feel is the direct result of having prioritized the comfort of "the grave" over the vulnerability of "the dawn," and realizing that until you are willing to get up, leave the swords behind, and walk forward into the unknown reality of your own, new potential, you will continue to be starved by the very, phantom catastrophe you have worked so hard to simulate.'
WHERE id = 38;
